      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Certificate level:**\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 5\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 2496280\n- Page total: 11\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 23 November 2021\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (from \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\n**Manufacturers:** Same as applicant — \"Applicant and Manufacturer\". The applicants table says \"Applicant and Manufacturer\". Should I add manufacturers? The rule says parse party layout variations. Since applicant and manufacturer are the same entity, I could add it in manufacturers[] as well. Let me add it.\n\nActually there are also production locations:\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, UK\n- ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd., No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201319, P.R. China\n- ABB Inc., 125 E. County Line Road, Warminster, PA 18974, USA\n\nThese are production locations, not necessarily \"manufacturers\" per se. I could include them as manufacturers or note them. Hmm. The schema has manufacturers[]. Production locations could reasonably be manufacturers. But to be safe, I'll include the manufacturer ABB Limited (as stated \"Applicant and Manufacturer\") and maybe include production locations as additional manufacturers entries. Actually production locations are distinct concept; the schema doesn't have a production_locations field. I could put them in manufacturers[] — that seems reasonable since they produce the meter. Let me include all three production locations plus note. Actually, the certificate explicitly says \"The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations\". I'll include them under manufacturers[] since that's the closest fit.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\" — R 49 water meters. The certificate says \"An electromagnetic water meter\". Category could be \"Water meter\".\n- Type designations: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 * — hmm. \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". The * footnote explains X, Y, Z. So type designations: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"? Actually \"FET4 *\" seems to be a separate type designation (maybe FET4 is the remote transmitter?). Let me record type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]. Actually the text: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\" with \"* With X being 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n\nThe \"*\" seems attached to both FEW4XY.Z and FET4? Actually \"FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\" — the asterisk after FET4 refers to the footnote. Probably the full designation is \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\" or the FET4 might be the transmitter type. Let me record both.\n\n- Module designation: not applicable? The instrument is a measuring system composed of flow sensor and flow transmitter. Hmm — there's mention of \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\". Components could be: flow sensor, flow transmitter, indicating device. But no explicit type designations for components. The certificate mentions \"indicating device\" characteristics in tables 6-9. I could add components: flow sensor and flow transmitter with roles. But they don't have type designations separate. Maybe I skip components or include with role only. Rule 8: don't fabricate. Components entries have type_designations[] — could be null? The schema says components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I think it's safer to not add components since no sub-assembly type designations are named. Actually the \"indicating device\" is named with characteristics (Tables 6-9). Hmm, I could add a component with role \"indicating device\" and characteristics captured... but the characteristics are config-level tables. Better to put tables 6-9 in matrix_tables.\n\n**Description:** \"An electromagnetic water meter\" — include in description.\n\n**Model family:** This cert covers a family of instruments: meter sizes DN40–DN1800, with variants by bore (reduced/virtual full bore/full bore), power supply (AC mains vs battery/renewable). The models axis: could use model_id like \"DN40\" ... \"DN1800\". The tables have meter size × accuracy class × power supply × bore configurations.\n\nI'll set model_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: one per DN size: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800.\n\nThat's 29 sizes.\n\n**Characteristics type_level (Table 1):**\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — hmm, this is a list of two values. Could put as value string \"1 and 2\" or list [1,2]. Schema says value can be number | {min,max} | string. I'll use string \"1 and 2\" or maybe list? Strictly the shape is value: number|{min,max}|string. I'll use \"1 and 2\"? Or better put in recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2]. Also type_level accuracy_class value: \"1 and 2\".\n- environmental_class: \"O (installed outdoors)\" → value \"O\" with maybe note. I'll include value \"O\" and a separate characteristic or description. Let me do value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"? Better: environmental_class: {value: \"O\"}. Hmm, the parenthetical clarifies installed outdoors. I'll keep value \"O (installed outdoors)\" as string? Cleaner: value \"O\". I might add a note... schema doesn't have notes on values. I'll keep \"O (installed outdoors)\" as string to preserve info. Actually canonical practice: keep it simple. value: \"O\".\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit \"°C\", note \"(for both flow sensor and flow transmitter)\"\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\" with range +0.1 to +50 °C. I can record water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C. The original says \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" → min 0.1, max 50.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (16 bar). Record value 1.6, unit \"MPa\". Could add note (16 bar) as separate characteristic max_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 bar. I'll include both: maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}; and maybe map: 16 bar. I'll add \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" only with MPa, and add a second characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_alt with 16 bar? Might be noisy. I'll include one: maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa.\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\" → value \"All positions\".\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have: U3 and D0\" — this is a config-dependent attribute! Use config_level:\n  - attribute: flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis: \"bore_size_and_bore_type\", values: [{condition: \"standard (all sizes except full bore DN250 and larger)\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}, {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}]\n  \n  Hmm, wait — original: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have: U3 and D0\". So config_level makes sense.\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → value true? Or string. value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"? I'll use value: true with... hmm. value shape allows string. reverse_flow: {value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"}? Simpler: value: true. I'll use true.\n- pressure_loss_class: config-dependent (reduced bore vs (virtual) full bore; and size ranges). This is a nested config. Could put in config_level with conditions:\n  - condition: \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"\n  - condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"\n  - condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"\n  - condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN65–DN200\", value: \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"\n  - condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"\n  \n  Values are strings like \"Δp 40\" — pressure loss class Δp40 corresponds to 0.40 bar. I could record as value \"Δp 40\" with footnote-like info. I'll do config_level entries with string values.\n- power_supply: split:\n  - AC mains: 85–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz → power_supply_voltage_ac: {min:85, max:240} V; power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz; power_supply_type_ac: \"AC mains\"\n  - Renewable energy power (DC powered): 6–32 V → power_supply_voltage_dc: {min:6, max:32} V\n  - Replaceable Battery: 2x 3.6 V (operating range: 2.9–3.6 V) → battery: number 2, battery_voltage: 3.6 V, battery_operating_voltage_range: {min:2.9, max:3.6} V.\n- software identification: multiple versions with checksums. Record as a config_level? Or matrix? I could add type_level entries: software_version values list. Shape constraints: value must be number|{min,max}|string. Three versions:\n  - software_version_1: \"03.00.03\", checksum \"B26AFE6B\"\n  - etc. I'll add config_level? Software versions are alternatives (OR). Could use components? No. I'll add type_level as a single string listing all? That loses structure. Alternative: use config_level with axis \"software_version\": [{condition: \"version 03.00.03\", value: \"B26AFE6B\"}]. Hmm, that's check sums. Or type_level: software_versions: value string \"03.00.03 (B26AFE6B); 03.00.04 (6E98963A); 03.02.00 (E12E5C04)\". I think a cleaner approach: three type_level characteristics:\n  - software_version: value \"03.00.03\" ... can't have three same attributes.\n  \n  I'll use config_level with attribute \"software\" and axis \"version\":\n  values: [{condition: \"03.00.03\", value: \"B26AFE6B\"}, ...] where value is checksum. Attribute: software_checksum, axis: software_version. That works nicely.\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" → value \"Type P (permanent)\".\n\n**Matrix tables (Tables 2-5):** Flow characteristics per meter size × accuracy class, for four configurations: reduced bore AC mains; reduced bore battery; full bore AC mains; full bore battery.\n\nColumns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1. Units m³/h for flow rates.\n\nAlso tables 6-9: indicating device characteristics per size group × config. Columns: meter_size, indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max, units m³.\n\nI need to enumerate all rows. Let me transcribe carefully.\n\n**Table 2: Reduced bore - AC mains powered**\n- DN40, class 1: Q1=0.05, Q2=0.08, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, ratio 500\n- DN40, class 2: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, ratio 1000\n- DN50, class 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, class 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n- DN100, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN125, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN125, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN150, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n- DN200, 1&2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n- DN250, 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n- DN300, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN350, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN350, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN400, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN450, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN500, 1: 8, 