 \"First issue.\" → revision \"0\"? Schema says revision string e.g. \"0\". \"Initial\" — I'll use \"initial\"? The example says revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). For \"Initial\" I'll put \"initial\". Hmm, maybe keep as \"Initial\" (as printed). I'll use \"initial\"? The doc says \"Initial\". I'll preserve \"initial\"... Let me just use \"initial\" lowercase? Better to preserve verbatim: \"Initial\". Fine.\n- \"1\", 2020-09-30, \"Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600\"\n- \"2\", 2020-12-24, \"Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\"\n- \"3\", 2021-08-02, \"Additional evaluation for extending temperature to –25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\"\n- \"4\", 2021-08-26, \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1200.\"\n- \"5\", 2021-11-23, \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\"\n\n**Footnotes:** The \"*\" note about X/Y/Z: marker \"*\", text \"With X being 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\" Include as footnote.\n\n**Type designations:** \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"AquaMaster4 FET4\". Hmm the text: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". So there appear to be two type patterns: FEW4XY.Z (flow sensor?) and FET4 (flow transmitter?). Given Y is \"integral/remote version, optionally sensor only\", FEW4 is likely the sensor and FET4 the transmitter (remote). I'll list both: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]. And module_designation: not applicable? The instrument comprises flow sensor and flow transmitter (mentioned in Table 1 and rev 3). Components: I could add:\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\"]}\n- {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]}\n\nIs that fabrication? The type designations given cover the whole meter; assigning FEW to sensor and FET to transmitter is inference. FEW = Flow Execute W? ABB AquaMaster: FEW = electromagnetic flow sensor; FET = remote transmitter (AquaMaster FET4... actually \"FET400\" series). The mapping is plausible but inferred. Rule 6: record components when cert names sub-assemblies. The cert names \"flow sensor\" and \"flow transmitter\" (in temperature range note and rev 3: \"E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter\"). But doesn't explicitly map type designations. I'll include components with roles only and put the type designations conservatively: both types under certified_type.type_designations; components with type_designations null? Schema expects arrays. I'll include components with role and type_designations as best-guess mapping? Rule 8 — don't fabricate. I'll include components without type mapping: type_designations: [] — hmm empty arrays. Maybe safer to omit components entirely. I'll omit.\n\nActually, I could include the indicating device as a component since Tables 6-9 are \"General characteristics of the indicating device\". Role: \"indicating device\". type_designations: []. Empty array... The schema says type_designations[]. I'll omit components to avoid noise. Hmm — but indicating device characteristics are captured in tables 6-9 anyway with names.\n\nI'll omit components. Note the type description mentions integral/remote — fine in footnote.\n\n**Category:** \"Water meter\" (R49 category). The cert says \"electromagnetic water meter\". Category: \"Water meter\".\n\n**Description:** \"An electromagnetic water meter. Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4, with X being 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\" Good.\n\n**Certificate number:** \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — include revision 5? The number field: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". I'll put number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Maybe revision separate? Schema doesn't have cert revision field; revision_history covers it. Keep number plain.\n\n**page_total:** 11.\n\n**oiml_issuer_id:** \"NL1\".\n\n**Units:** degree Celsius symbol \"°C\". unit_id: I could fill \"u:degree_Celsius\" — rule says leave null unless certain. I'm fairly certain of degree_Celsius but the instruction says leave null unless certain; I'll set a few common ones? The example shows \"u:degree_Celsius\". To be safe, I'll leave unit_id null everywhere — the caller looks up unit_symbol. Yes, instruction: \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain of the unitsdb ID.\" I'll leave null.\n\n**Structured values — check Table 1 extraction:**\n\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — hmm, string. Fine.\n- environmental_class: {value: \"O\"} — include note? I'll add value \"O\" and a separate type_level characteristic? I'll keep value string \"O (installed outdoors)\". Slight tension with clean values, but preserves info. Actually, environmental_class \"O\" is the class; \"installed outdoors\" is its meaning. I'll do value: \"O\".\n\nLet me add \"environment\": not needed.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E2\"}\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- temperature_range_ambient: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}; also map_to_bar 16: add characteristic \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\"? I'll add \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" with MPa only. Or include both in one entry? Shape is single value. I'll add second entry: attribute \"maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent\": {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}. Hmm \"equivalent\" is fabricating label slightly. It's fine — parenthetical conversion. I'll include it.\n\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions\"}\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"} — hmm. I'll use {value: true}? The statement: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". value true is clean. I'll do value: true.\n- checking_facilities: {value: \"Type P (permanent)\"} with description \"automatic checking facility\"? Value string \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"? I'll use value \"Type P (permanent)\" — keep attribute name \"automatic_checking_facility\". Actually attribute \"checking_facility\": {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic\"}. I'll go: checking_facility: {value: \"Type P (permanent)\"}.\n- software: config_level as decided: attribute \"software_checksum\", axis \"software_version\", values [{condition: \"03.00.03\", value: \"B26AFE6B\"}, {condition: \"03.00.04\", value: \"6E98963A\"}, {condition: \"03.02.00\", value: \"E12E5C04\"}]. Values here are strings without units — shape {condition, value}. Fine.\n- power supply entries:\n  - power_supply_type: {value: \"AC mains, renewable energy (DC) or replaceable battery\"}? Maybe simpler: three characteristics:\n    - ac_mains_voltage: {value: {min: 85, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n    - ac_mains_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n    - dc_supply_voltage (renewable energy power): {value: {min: 6, max: 32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n    - battery_count: {value: 2}\n    - battery_cell_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n    - battery_operating_voltage_range: {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - Also power_supply_type entries: {value: \"AC mains\"} / \"DC\" / battery? I'll add power_supply_type: {value: \"AC mains or DC (renewable energy) or replaceable battery\"} as string. Fine.\n\n- pressure_loss_class: config_level:\n  attribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"bore_type_and_meter_size\", values:\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"} — wait, Δp 40 corresponds to 0.40 bar. Convert comma: 0,40 → 0.40 (0.4). I'll write \"Δp 40 (0.4 bar)\". Keep as string values.\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"virtual or full bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.1 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"virtual or full bore, DN65 to DN200\", value: \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"virtual or full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.1 bar)\"}\n  \n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: config_level:\n  - {condition: \"all sizes except full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 × DN upstream, 0 × DN downstream)\"} — the \"×\" is fine (multiplication sign, not LaTeX). Original \"0 x DN\". I'll keep \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\".\n  - {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n\nWait — original: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have: U3 and D0\". So the exception is full bore DN250+. Condition 1: \"except full bore DN250 and larger\" → default. Condition 2: \"full bore DN250 and larger\".\n\n**Accuracy classes in tables:** \"1 & 2\" rows mean both classes share the row. In matrix rows I'll record accuracy_class: \"1 & 2\"? Or [1,2]? Rows are objects; accuracy_class value could be \"1 & 2\" string or array. I'll use string \"1 & 2\"? Or \"1 and 2\"? Keep \"1 & 2\" as in doc. Hmm — for machine-readability, maybe \"1,2\" array? Rows must be objects; I'll use \"1 & 2\" string to be faithful. Actually let me use string \"1 and 2\"? I'll keep \"1 & 2\".\n\n**Model list:** DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800. That's 29 models.\n\n**Matrix tables definitions:**\n\nFlow tables (2, 3, 4, 5):\n- name: \"Table 2: General characteristics of the family — reduced bore, AC mains powered\"\n- columns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4