00, DN350, DN400, DN450], 9999999, 9999999, 0.01}\n6. {[DN500, DN600], 9999999, 9999999, 0.1}\n\nTable 8 (full bore, AC mains):\n1. {[DN40, DN50], 9999999, 99999, 0.0001}\n2. {[DN65], 9999999, 999999, 0.0001}\n3. {[DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150], 9999999, 999999, 0.001}\n4. {[DN200, DN250, DN300], 9999999, 9999999, 0.01}\n5. {[DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600], 9999999, 9999999, 0.1}\n6. {[DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200], 99999999, 99999999, 0.1}\n7. {[DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800], 999999999, 99999999, 1}\n\nTable 9 (full bore, battery):\n1. {[DN40, DN50], 9999999, 99999, 0.001}\n2. {[DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125], 9999999, 999999, 0.001}\n3. {[DN150], 9999999, 999999, 0.01}\n4. {[DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500], 9999999, 9999999, 0.01}\n5. {[DN600], 99999999, 9999999, 0.1}\n6. {[DN700, DN750, DN800], 99999999, 99999999, 0.1}\n7. {[DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650], 999999999, 99999999, 1}\n\nColumn names for indicating tables: meter_sizes, indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max. Units: null, \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\".\n\nFootnotes: the \"*\" footnote. marker: \"*\", text.\n\nDescription of flow-rate selection note: I'll add to each flow table description: \"Flow rates Q1–Q4 can be freely chosen provided: Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from OIML R49-1:2013 §4.1; Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values and Q4/Q3 = 1.25; Q3/Q1 ≥ 40.\"\n\nNow assemble final JSON. Watch JSON validity — no trailing commas.\n\nAlso include certificate.date_issued: \"2021-11-23\", oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\", member_state: \"The Netherlands\", page_total: 11, scheme: \"A\", project_number: \"2496280\".\n\nIssuing authority: name \"NMi Certin B.V.\", address_lines, person_responsible \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\", phone \"+31 88 636 2332\", email \"certin@nmi.nl\", website \"www.nmi.nl\", oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]\n\nWait — should FET4 include the asterisk? \"FET4 *\" — the * is a footnote marker referring to the X/Y/Z note which applies to the whole \"FEW4XY.Z and FET4\" family? The note defines X, Y, Z which appear in FEW4XY.Z. The * after FET4... In the original PDF likely \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\". The footnote explains variable letters. I'll keep designations clean: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"AquaMaster4 FET4\" and put the footnote in footnotes[]. Add footnote_markers to type_designations? No field for that. Fine.\n\n- module_designation: null? The instrument is a complete measuring instrument; module designation not mentioned. Omit or null. I'll set null.\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter. Type AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4, where X is 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y is 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z is R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: 29 entries.\n\nLet me count models again: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800 → count: 40,50,65,80 (4), 100,125,150,200 (8), 250,300,350,400 (12), 450,500,600 (15), 700,750,800 (18), 900,1000,1050,1100,1200 (23), 1350,1400,1500,1600,1650,1800 (29). Yes 29.\n\nHmm — should model_id reflect config too? I'll keep size. But maybe add attributes to each model? Like {\"model_id\": \"DN40\", \"attributes\": {}}. Keep {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"} only? The R137 example had {\"model_id\": \"G1.6\"}. I'll add a label maybe. Keep minimal.\n\nrecommendation: {\"id\": \"R49-1\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]}\n\nHmm, accuracy classes as numbers 1 and 2 — fine.\n\ntest_reports:\n1. {id: \"NMi-2254799-01\", date: \"2020-08-14\", pages: 235, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n2. {id: \"NMi-2254799-03\", date: \"2020-09-30\", pages: 44, role: \"test_report\"}\n3. {id: \"NMi-2254799-04\", date: \"2020-12-24\", pages: 180, role: \"test_report\"}\n4. {id: \"NMi-2564429-01\", date: \"2021-08-02\", pages: 26, role: \"test_report\"}\n5. {id: \"NMi-2496280-01\", date: \"2021-08-21\"?? No: 26 August 2021 → \"2021-08-26\", pages: 29, role: \"test_report\"}\n6. {id: \"NMi-2496280-02\", date: \"2021-11-23\", pages: 25, role: \"test_report\"}\n7. {id: \"T11851-5\", role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\nrevision_history:\n1. {revision: \"initial\", date: \"2020-08-14\", changes: \"First issue.\"} — the doc says revision \"Initial\". I'll use \"initial\"? Keep \"Initial\"? I'll use \"initial\" hmm. The schema example: revision string e.g. \"0\". I'll use \"initial\" lowercase for consistency? Verbatim \"Initial\" is also a string. I'll use \"initial\".\n\nHmm, actually preserving \"Initial\" verbatim is safest. But then revisions are \"Initial\",\"1\",\"2\"... consistent strings. Fine, \"initial\" vs \"Initial\" — I'll go \"initial\" (normalized). Eh, whatever — \"initial\".\n\n2. {revision: \"1\", date: \"2020-09-30\", changes: \"Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600\"}\n3. {revision: \"2\", date: \"2020-12-24\", changes: \"Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\"}\n4. {revision: \"3\", date: \"2021-08-02\", changes: \"Additional evaluation for extending temperature to -25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\"}\n5. {revision: \"4\", date: \"2021-08-26\", changes: \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1200.\"}\n6. {revision: \"5\", date: \"2021-11-23\", changes: \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\"}\n\nfootnotes: [{marker: \"*\", text: \"With X being 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"}]\n\ncharacteristics.config_level:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore_type_and_meter_size\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"all sizes except full bore DN250 and larger\", \"value\": \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore DN250 and larger\", \"value\": \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss_class\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore_type_and_meter_size\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", \"value\": \"Delta-p 40 (0.40 bar)\"},\n      ...\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"software_checksum\",\n    \"axis\": \"software_version\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"03.00.03\", \"value\": \"B26AFE6B\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"03.00.04\", \"value\": \"6E98963A\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"03.02.00\", \"value\": \"E12E5C04\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nFor Δp — the \"Δ\" character is fine (not LaTeX). I'll use \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". Config_level values shape: the schema example for config_level values: {condition, value}. I'll follow that (no unit wrapper for string values; for numeric-ish like checksum strings fine). Should config values use the structured form {value, unit_symbol}? Rule 5 says \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". Hmm — so config_level values should be {condition, value, unit_symbol}. For string class values, unit_symbol null. I'll include unit_symbol: null in each to conform. OK.\n\nAlso should I include type_level values with unit_id? All null. And footnote_markers [] or omitted? The shape shows footnote_markers in the object; I'll include only when present (footnote \"*\" applies to type designations, not Table 1 values). Omit empty arrays for cleanliness — or include for strict shape? Rule 2 shows the shape with footnote_markers; I'll include footnote_markers only where relevant, since rule 8 says omit absent fields.\n\nNow, \"water_temperature_class\": \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\" — I split into class T50 and range. Good.\n\nAlso \"environmental_class\" — I'll value \"O\" and add note within description? I'll use value \"O (installed outdoors)\". Decided.\n\nLet me also double check Table 2 DN65: \"0,063 / 0,1008 / 63 / 78,75 / 1000\" → Q1 0.063, Q2 0.1008. Yes.\n\nTable 4 DN50: 0,126 → 0.126, 0,2016 → 0.2016.\n\nTable 5 DN50: 0,504 → 0.504, 0,8064 → 0.8064; class 2: 0,252 → 0.252, 0,4032 → 0.4032.\n\nNow output size: ~113 flow rows + 25 indicating rows ≈ large but manageable.\n\nOne more consideration: should I include \"matrix_tables.column_units\"? The R137 example includes column_units. Yes, include.\n\nAlso each table: name, columns, column_units, rows, description, footnotes. footnotes [] omitted.\n\nDescriptions:\n- Table 2: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments — reduced bore, AC mains powered. Flow rates Q1–Q4 can be freely chosen provided: Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from OIML R49-1:2013 clause 4.1; Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values and Q4/Q3 = 1.25; Q3/Q1 is at least 40.\"\nSimilar for others.\n\nAccuracy class representation in rows: numbers 1 / 2, string \"1 & 2\" for combined. OK.\n\nNow — one concern: `q1`, `q2` etc. Fine.\n\nLet me also add to certified_type maybe \"model_family\" only at top level. Right.\n\nLet me now also decide manufacturers array:\n1. {name: \"ABB Limited\", address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}\n2. {name: \"ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd.\", address_lines: [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway\", \"Pudong New District\", \"Shanghai, 201319\", \"P.R. China\"]}\n3. {name: \"ABB Inc.\", address_lines: [\"125 E. County Line Road\", \"Warminster, PA 18974\", \"United States of America\"]}\n\nAddress line splitting: keep close to source: \"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201319, P.R. China\" — I'll split: [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District\", \"Shanghai, 201319\", \"P.R. China\"]. Fine.\n\nApplicants: ABB Limited.\n\nNow assemble. Also certificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\".\n\nDouble-check the issuing authority \"Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" — title not given; omit person_title.\n\nAlso should I include certificate-level \"scheme\": \"A\" — yes.\n\nWrite the JSON now. Be meticulous with commas.\n\nOne more thought on type_level attribute names:\n- measuring_principle\n- accuracy_class\n- environmental_class\n- electromagnetic_environment\n- mechanical_class\n- temperature_range_ambient\n- water_temperature_class\n- water_temperature_range\n- maximum_admissible_pressure\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_bar? I'll name it \"maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent_bar\"? Simpler: skip? I'll include as \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}. Hmm redundant label \"bar\". \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alt\"? I'll do \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\" — clear enough.\n\nActually let me reconsider — cleanest: maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} and that's it; the (16 bar) is conversion noise. But preservation... I'll include the bar one; harmless.\n\n- orientation\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"}? Or boolean true. I'll do {\"value\": true}. Attribute name \"reverse_flow_measurement\"? Keep \"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": true}. Hmm, boolean true conveys \"sensor intended to measure reverse flow\" = reverse flow measurement supported = true. OK.\n- checking_facility: {value: \"Type P (permanent)\"} — maybe include \"automatic checking\" in attribute name: \"automatic_checking_facility\": {value: \"Type P (permanent)\"}. Good.\n- ac_mains_voltage\n- ac_mains_frequency\n- dc_powered_supply_voltage\n- replaceable_battery_cells\n- replaceable_battery_voltage\n- replaceable_battery_operating_range\n\nAlso software versions as type_level string? I put config_level checksum. Maybe also a type_level \"software_version\": can't list 3 values. config covers it. OK.\n\nReady to write output. Length is fine; go.\n\nAlso note: \"This Certificate relates...\" boilerplate — ignore.\n\nFor matrix_tables names: \"Table 2 - General characteristics of the family of instruments: reduced bore, AC mains powered\" etc.\n\nFinal JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {...},\n  \"issuing_authority\": {...},\n  \"applicants\": [...],\n  \"manufacturers\": [...],\n  \"certified_type\": {...},\n  \"model_family\": {...},\n  \"characteristics\": {\"type_level\": {...}, \"config_level\": [...]},\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...8 tables...],\n  \"recommendation\": {...},\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [...],\n  \"footnotes\": [...]\n}\n\nCheck: characteristics.type_level is an object of label→structured value. Good.\n\nWrite it.",
